  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>RunOwnershipError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Run Ownership Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      Protocol Engine currently owns the Flex for a prepared or active run.

      Read status through the connector as usual, but do not issue an independent
      SiLA hardware command. Stop the Protocol Engine run, or add the operation at
      an explicit UNITELABS mutation checkpoint through the controlled run-mutation
      HTTP endpoint, then retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>NotHomedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Not Homed Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The robot&apos;s position is unknown, so the move was refused.

      Home the robot (or the affected mount) before requesting a move. If a halt
      interrupted gripper motion, fully home the robot and then run HomeJaw before
      resuming other hardware actuation.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>HardwareBusyError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Hardware Busy Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The shared Flex hardware stayed busy longer than the configured lock timeout.

      Another SiLA client, an HTTP/Protocol Engine operation, or a long-running module
      action currently holds the hardware. Wait for that operation to finish (watch the
      RobotStatusProvider Status property) and retry, or raise lock_timeout_s in the
      connector configuration.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>OperationHaltedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Operation Halted Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The operation was aborted because an emergency stop or halt was issued while it ran.

      The robot position is no longer trusted. Clear the cause of the halt, fully
      re-home the robot, follow the RecoveryGuidance reported by RobotStatusProvider,
      and then re-issue the operation.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>RecoveryRequiredError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Recovery Required Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The connector refused the operation because an earlier failure left the robot
      recovery-gated.

      The error message lists the ordered recovery actions (for example a full re-home,
      TipController.ReconcileTipState, or GripperController.HomeJaw). The same guidance
      is available at any time from the RobotStatusProvider Status property.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>HardwareError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Hardware Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The Flex hardware reported an error that has no more specific defined error.

      The message preserves the Opentrons error code, name, and detail so the cause
      can be diagnosed or reported to vendor support. Inspect the robot, resolve the
      reported condition, re-home, and retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>MovementOutOfBoundsError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Movement Out Of Bounds Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The requested move would take the mount outside the deck working volume.

      Check the target coordinates against the Flex deck envelope and retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>StallDetectedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Stall Detected Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      A stall or collision was detected during the move; motion was halted.

      Clear any obstruction, then re-home before continuing. The underlying
      hardware message is preserved to aid diagnosis.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>MachineErrorStateError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Machine Error State Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The move was accepted but the robot has silently entered a hardware error state.

      A movement command can return without raising while the machine has already
      entered an error state (for example the E-stop became engaged mid-move). This
      error surfaces that hidden condition so callers never treat such a move as
      successful. Clear the fault (release the E-stop), then re-home before
      continuing. The underlying hardware state is preserved to aid diagnosis.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>GripperNotAttachedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Gripper Not Attached Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      No gripper is attached, so the requested gripper action cannot run.

      Attach the Flex gripper to the rear mount and re-scan instruments before retrying.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>GripActionError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Grip Action Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The gripper failed to complete a grip, ungrip, or home-jaw action.

      The underlying hardware message is preserved so the failure can be diagnosed.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>LabwareMovementNotAllowedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Labware Movement Not Allowed Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The requested labware move is unsafe for the declared deck or module state.

      Reconcile deck occupancy, open any required module lid or latch, clear the
      gripper path, and submit a new movement plan before retrying.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>DestinationOccupiedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Destination Occupied Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The requested destination is occupied in the supplied deck snapshot.

      Remove or relocate the occupying item, refresh the deck occupancy snapshot,
      and retry. The connector will not overwrite an occupied location.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>LabwareNotPickedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Labware Not Picked Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      Gripper jaw-width verification indicates that the labware was not picked up.

      Inspect the source alignment and labware geometry, clear obstructions, then
      fully home the robot and gripper jaw before retrying.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>LabwareNotPlacedError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Labware Not Placed Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The gripper did not complete a verified placement at the destination.

      Inspect both the gripper and destination, reconcile the physical deck state,
      then fully home the robot and gripper jaw before any further movement.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
</Feature>
